// Arena named-result probe: the RETURN boundary, via the one return shape
// that carries no values for the return sink to walk.
//
// `id` hands back a parameter-derived pointer through a NAMED result and a
// BARE `return`. ReturnStmtNode->values is empty there, so param_escape's
// return sink never fires and return_escapes stays false.
//
// The parameter is still marked, by a different sink: LocalEnv is seeded from
// f->param_names only, so a named result is NOT a local, and `p = q` is a
// store to a non-local. That fires the store-escape sink, `id`'s param 0 is
// retained, and block_escape routes the call site to the heap.
//
// So this shape is sound today, and it is sound for a reason that is easy to
// break: anyone who later registers named results as locals removes the ONLY
// sink covering it, because the return sink cannot see a bare return. This
// probe fails in that world, which is the point of keeping it.
//
// `kept` is read after the block frees its arena.
package main

type T struct {
	x int
}

var kept *T

func id(q *T) (p *T) {
	p = q
	return
}

func main() {
	arena {
		a := &T{x: 42}
		kept = id(a)
	}
	println(kept.x)
}
